lel to the \\(x\\)-axis and therefore **not sunny**.  \n* \\(L_{3}\\) has slope \\(1\\); it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(m=0\\)), not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(m=\\infty\\)), and not parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (which has slope \\(-1\\)). Hence \\(L_{3}\\) **is sunny**.  \n\nThe three lines are clearly distinct.\n\n---\n\n#### Verification of coverage  \n\nWe check each point of \\(S\\) individually.\n\n| Point \\((a,b)\\) | Lies on which line(s)? | Verification |\n|-----------------|------------------------|--------------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\) | \\(x=1\\) (true), \\(y=1\\) (true) |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) | \\(x=1\\) (true) |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) | \\(x=1\\) (true) |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) | \\(y=1\\) (true) |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) | \\(y=1\\) (true) |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(L_{3}\\) | \\(y=x\\) gives \\(2=2\\) (true) |\n\nAll six points are covered.\n\n---\n\n#### Counting sunny lines  \n\nAmong \\(\\{L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\}\\) only \\(L_{3}\\) satisfies the definition of a sunny line. Hence the number of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\nk = 1 .\n\\]\n\nThus a configuration with \\(n=3\\) lines and exactly \\(k=1\\) sunny line exists.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, }k=1\\text{ is attainable.}}\n\\]"
