n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{pair} & \\text{slope}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)-(1,2),\\;(1,1)-(1,3),\\;(2,2)-(2,1) & \\infty\\\\\n(1,1)-(2,1),\\;(1,2)-(2,2),\\;(2,1)-(3,1) & 0\\\\\n(1,1)-(2,2) & 1\\\\\n(1,1)-(3,1) & \\tfrac12\\\\\n(1,2)-(3,1) & -\\tfrac12\\\\\n(1,3)-(2,1) & -2\\\\\n\\text{all other pairs} & -1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThe only slopes that are **not** \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) are \\(1,\\tfrac12,-\\tfrac12,-2\\); each of these occurs for exactly one pair of points. Hence no three points of \\(S\\) are collinear with a sunny slope. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nIf a non‑sunny line contains three points of \\(S\\), then the three points of \\(S\\) that are *not* on this line cannot be covered by two sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe only non‑sunny directions are horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)), and slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  \nAmong the six points of \\(S\\) the only lines of these three directions that contain three points are  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:x+y=4 .\n\\]\n\nWe treat each case separately.\n\n*Case \\(L_{1}:x=1\\).*  \nPoints on \\(L_{1}\\) are \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \nThe remaining points are \\(\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nSlopes between any two of them are \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nThus any line through two of these points is non‑sunny, so a sunny line can contain at most one of them. Covering three points would need three sunny lines, contradicting the hypothesis of exactly two sunny lines.\n\n*Case \\(L_{2}:y=1\\).*  \nPoints on \\(L_{2}\\) are \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \nThe remaining points are \\(\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}\\).  \nAgain every pair among them has slope \\(\\infty\\), \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently a sunny line can contain at most one of them, and three sunny lines would be required.\n\n*Case \\(L_{3}:x+y=4\\).*  \nPoints on \\(L_{3}\\) are \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nThe remaining points are \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAll pairwise slopes are \\(\\infty\\), \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\); the same argument as above shows that two sunny lines cannot cover the three points.\n\nIn every possible case the three points outside the non‑sunny line need at least three sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a configuration of three distinct lines covering all points of \\(S\\) with exactly two sunny lines.  \n\nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line covers at most two points of \\(S\\); therefore the two sunny lines together cover at most four points. Consequently the remaining (at least) two points must lie on the single non‑sunny line.\n\nIf the non‑sunny line contained only two points of \\(S\\), the two sunny lines would have to cover the other four points. Since each sunny line can cover at most two points, they would have to cover exactly two points each, which would force the four points to be partitioned into two pairs each having a sunny slope. A direct inspection of the slopes listed in Lemma 1 shows that no such partition exists (the only pairs with sunny slopes are \\((1,1)-(2,2),\\;(1,1)-(3,1),\\;(1,2)-(3,1),\\;(1,3)-(2,1)\\), and none of these four points can be split into two disjoint sunny pairs). Hence the non‑sunny line cannot contain only two points.\n\nThus the non‑sunny line must contain three points of \\(S\\). By Lemma 2 the three points not on this line cannot be covered by the two sunny lines, a contradiction.\n\nTherefore a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{No such configuration exists; for }n=3\\text{, }k=2\\text{ is impossible}.}\n\\]"
