\) (exactly two sunny lines)\n\nTake a non‑sunny vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:\\;x=2 .\n\\]\n\nIt covers \\((2,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\).\n\nChoose two sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{2}&:\\;y=-\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac52 \n      &&\\text{(through }(1,2)\\text{ and }(3,1),\\ \\text{slope }-\\tfrac12),\\\\\nL_{3}&:\\;y=-2x+5\n      &&\\text{(through }(1,3)\\text{ and }(2,1),\\ \\text{slope }-2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Slopes:* \\(L_{2}\\) and \\(L_{3}\\) have slopes \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and \\(-2\\), both sunny; \\(L_{1}\\) is vertical (non‑sunny). Hence exactly two sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage:*  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{Point}&\\text{Containing line}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)&\\text{none yet— but }(1,1)\\text{ lies on }L_{2}?\\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nWe must check each point:\n\n- \\((1,1)\\): substitute into \\(L_{2}\\): \\(-\\tfrac12\\cdot1+\\tfrac52 =2\\neq1\\); into \\(L_{3}\\): \\(-2\\cdot1+5=3\\neq1\\); into \\(L_{1}\\): \\(x=2\\neq1\\). Thus \\((1,1)\\) is **not** covered.  \nWe need to adjust the construction.\n\nCorrected construction:\n\nUse the non‑sunny line \\(L_{1}: y=1\\) (horizontal) instead of a vertical line.  \nThen choose two sunny lines that together cover the remaining points.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:\\;y=1 \\quad(\\text{non‑sunny}),\\\\\nL_{2}&:\\;y=-\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac52 \\quad(\\text{through }(1,2),(3,1)),\\\\\nL_{3}&:\\;y=-2x+5 \\quad(\\text{through }(1,3),(2,1)).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Slopes:* \\(L_{1}\\) horizontal (non‑sunny); \\(L_{2},L_{3}\\) sunny.\n\n*Coverage verification:*\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{Point}&\\text{Line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)&L_{1}\\\\\n(2,1)&L_{1}\\text{ and }L_{3}\\\\\n(3,1)&L_{1}\\text{ and }L_{2}\\\\\n(1,2)&L_{2}\\\\\n(2,2)&\\text{none yet— check }L_{2}: -\\tfrac12\\cdot2+\\tfrac52=1.5\\neq2; L_{3}:-2\\cdot2+5=1\\neq2; L_{1}:y=1\\neq2.\\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nPoint \\((2,2)\\) is still uncovered.  \n\nWe therefore need a different set of three lines.  \n\nA successful